{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. ___ from the surgery team. I understand you came in with abdominal pain. Can you tell me where it hurts and what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:16:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s mostly in the upper middle, but also on both sides up high. It’s been about five days and it feels different than my ulcer pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:17:05",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Different how—more sharp, more constant, or related to eating?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:17:55",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "More constant and kind of gnawing, and I’ve been nauseated. No vomiting though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:18:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any diarrhea or changes in your stools?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:19:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, diarrhea. And my stools have been darker. That’s what scared me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:20:05",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important to mention. We’ll check for bleeding and monitor your blood counts. Any dizziness, fainting, or racing heart?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:20:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little lightheaded earlier, but I haven’t passed out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:21:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We’ll start IV fluids, nausea medicine, and pain control. Given your Roux-en-Y bypass and history of marginal ulcer, we’ll also involve Gastroenterology."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:22:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m worried it’s C. diff again. I had that last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:23:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a reasonable concern with diarrhea. We’ll send stool tests for C. difficile, and also check for H. pylori because of the ulcer history."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:24:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Am I going to need surgery?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right now, nothing suggests an emergency surgery, but we need to evaluate carefully. We’ll start with labs and imaging to make sure there’s no obstruction or free air."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2169-06-19 16:33:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re getting an abdominal X-ray now to look for signs of blockage or a perforation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2169-06-19 17:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your abdominal X-ray doesn’t show free air and doesn’t show a high-grade bowel obstruction. That’s reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2169-06-19 17:11:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So why does it hurt so much?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2169-06-19 17:12:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Pain can still come from inflammation or ulcers even when the X-ray is normal. Your bloodwork so far looks stable, and we’ll keep trending it because of the dark stools."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2169-06-19 17:13:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Are you checking my blood count for bleeding?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2169-06-19 17:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’ll monitor your hematocrit and hemoglobin. We also checked a stool test here that was positive for blood, so we’re taking that seriously."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2169-06-19 17:15:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on pantoprazole and sucralfate already. Is that not enough?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2169-06-19 17:16:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Sometimes ulcers at the gastrojejunal connection need higher-dose acid suppression. We’ll likely increase your pantoprazole to twice daily and continue sucralfate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2169-06-19 18:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re going to admit you to the surgical ward for monitoring, IV fluids, and to coordinate an endoscopy with GI."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2169-06-19 18:06:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I be able to eat?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2169-06-19 18:07:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "For now we’ll keep you on a cautious plan until GI evaluates you—then we’ll advance as tolerated. The priority is controlling pain and nausea and making sure there’s no bleeding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2169-06-20 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How was your night? Any vomiting or worsening pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2169-06-20 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No vomiting. Pain comes in waves. The nausea is still there."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2169-06-20 08:17:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep IV fluids going and adjust your pain and nausea meds. GI is planning an EGD today to look at the anastomosis and check for ulcers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2169-06-20 08:18:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What exactly is an EGD again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2169-06-20 08:19:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a camera test—an endoscope goes through your mouth to look at the esophagus, stomach pouch, and the connection to the small intestine. They can take small biopsies if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2169-06-20 08:20:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is it dangerous with my bypass?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2169-06-20 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s commonly done after bypass. There are risks with any procedure, but it’s generally safe and it gives us the best information about ulcers and inflammation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2169-06-20 11:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Before the EGD, just confirming: you use CPAP at home for sleep apnea, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2169-06-20 11:10:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, every night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2169-06-20 11:11:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. We’ll make sure anesthesia and recovery staff know, since sedation can affect breathing. We’ll monitor you closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re back from the EGD. How are you feeling right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:45:40",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Sleepy. My throat is sore. Did they find anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They saw gastritis—irritation of the lining—and at the gastrojejunal connection they saw a new small ulcer. They also saw that your larger known ulcer there looks like it’s improving, and one smaller ulcer they’d seen before is no longer visible."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:48:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I have another ulcer. Is that why my stool is dark?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:49:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "An ulcer can cause bleeding, yes. Your blood counts have been stable, and we’re continuing to monitor them. If you notice black tarry stool or bright red blood, tell us right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:50:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What’s the plan to treat it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:51:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re increasing pantoprazole to 40 mg twice a day and continuing sucralfate four times a day. GI also wants an H. pylori stool antigen test to check for active infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:52:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "They mentioned something about a biopsy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2169-06-20 12:53:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They took small biopsies during the EGD. If anything important comes back, GI will contact you and we’ll also review it with you while you’re here if it results in time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2169-06-20 13:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But you said the findings might not explain how bad the pain is?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2169-06-20 13:11:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right. Because your pain feels out of proportion, GI recommended a CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast to look for other causes like obstruction or other complications."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2169-06-20 14:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re also sending stool testing for C. difficile given your diarrhea and prior infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2169-06-20 18:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your CT scan is back. It does not show a bowel obstruction. It also doesn’t show contrast in the gastric remnant, but the oral contrast had already moved through the bowel by the time the images were taken, so it’s not pointing to a blockage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2169-06-20 18:11:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So nothing is wrong on the CT?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2169-06-20 18:12:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Nothing that explains severe pain like an obstruction. There was a small area in the left kidney that could represent a very small infection or an area of decreased blood flow. The radiologist suggested correlating with your urinalysis, and your urine test didn’t show signs of infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2169-06-20 18:13:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do I need antibiotics for my kidney?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2169-06-20 18:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "At this point, with no urinary symptoms and a reassuring urinalysis, we’re not starting antibiotics just based on that CT finding. We’ll keep an eye on it and on your symptoms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2169-06-20 20:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "When will I know about the C. diff test?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2169-06-20 20:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The first C. difficile toxin test came back negative today. We’ll repeat if needed based on symptoms, but so far it doesn’t look like C. diff."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2169-06-21 08:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How’s your pain today compared with yesterday?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2169-06-21 08:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still there, but the IV morphine helped. I’m nervous about switching to pills."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2169-06-21 08:42:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s understandable. We need a plan that works by mouth before you go home. We’ll transition carefully and make sure you’re comfortable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2169-06-21 11:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We advanced your diet today. Let us know how you do after eating—sometimes ulcers cause pain after meals."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2169-06-21 13:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "After lunch I had worse pain for maybe 45 minutes, then it eased up. I could still keep it down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2169-06-21 13:06:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That pattern fits with ulcer irritation. The fact you tolerated the meal overall is a good sign. We’ll keep the pantoprazole twice daily and sucralfate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2169-06-21 13:07:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The oral Roxicet isn’t touching the pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2169-06-21 13:08:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for telling me. If it’s not effective, we’ll switch to a different oral pain medication. We can try oral hydromorphone and see if it controls your pain better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The new pill helped a lot more. I’m less scared now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. We’ll keep it as needed and aim for the lowest dose that keeps you functional—walking, drinking, and eating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:22:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the dark stools? Am I still bleeding?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:23:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve been checking your hematocrit. It dipped slightly with hydration but has stabilized and is back in the normal range. That suggests no ongoing significant bleeding right now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:24:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the H. pylori test?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your stool H. pylori antigen test is negative, which means we don’t see evidence of an active H. pylori infection contributing to the ulcer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So why do I keep getting ulcers?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2169-06-21 16:27:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "After gastric bypass, ulcers can form at the connection site for several reasons, and sometimes we don’t find a single trigger. The key is strong acid suppression, protecting the lining with sucralfate, and close follow-up with repeat endoscopy to confirm healing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 70,
      "time": "2169-06-21 18:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My diarrhea is better today. Does that mean it’s not C. diff?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 71,
      "time": "2169-06-21 18:31:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It supports that. Also, we repeated the C. difficile toxin test today and it’s negative again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 72,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "This morning you look more comfortable. Are you able to eat and drink enough?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 73,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. I’m eating regular food, just smaller amounts. Pain is controlled with the Dilaudid pills."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 74,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:02:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. You’re also walking and urinating normally, and your vital signs have been stable. We’re planning discharge later today if everything stays steady."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 75,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:03:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What do I go home on? I’m confused about the pantoprazole dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 76,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:04:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll take pantoprazole 40 mg twice a day now—morning and evening. Continue sucralfate 1 gram four times a day, and crush it as you’ve been doing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 77,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:05:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "How long will I need the stronger dose?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 78,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:06:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "GI wants you on the twice-daily dose until your follow-up endoscopy in about six weeks, and then they’ll decide based on healing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 79,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:07:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pain medicine—will I get enough? I don’t want to be stuck in pain at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 80,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:08:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll prescribe oral hydromorphone to take as needed for pain. It’s for short-term use. Don’t drive or operate machinery while taking it, and use the smallest amount that works."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 81,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:09:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What should make me come back to the ER?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 82,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:10:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Come back if you have signs of bleeding—black tarry stools, bright red blood in stool or vomit—dizziness, weakness, feeling like you might faint, or a fast heart rate. Also return if you can’t keep down fluids or meds, or if pain worsens or changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 83,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:11:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do I need to do anything about the kidney spot they saw?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 84,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:12:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right now, no specific treatment since your urine test didn’t suggest infection and you don’t have urinary symptoms. If you develop burning with urination, fever, or flank pain, you should call or come in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 85,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:13:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "When do I follow up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 86,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:14:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll follow up with Gastroenterology for a repeat EGD in about six weeks. And please call your surgeon’s office to make a follow-up appointment within two weeks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 87,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:15:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I keep taking my other meds and vitamins?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 88,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:16:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—resume your regular home medications unless we specifically changed them. Continue your multivitamin, vitamin D, B12 weekly, ursodiol, and trazodone as prescribed, and crush pills as instructed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 89,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:17:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m still scared this will happen again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 90,
      "time": "2169-06-22 09:18:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s completely understandable. The repeat endoscopy is important to confirm healing, and staying consistent with the pantoprazole and sucralfate gives you the best chance to prevent recurrence."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 91,
      "time": "2169-06-22 19:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Before you go, last check: pain controlled on oral meds, tolerating regular diet, walking independently, and no new bleeding symptoms?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 92,
      "time": "2169-06-22 19:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. I feel ready. I’ll call if I see black stools or get dizzy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 93,
      "time": "2169-06-22 19:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. We’ll discharge you home now with pantoprazole twice daily, sucralfate, and hydromorphone as needed, and the follow-up plan we discussed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2169-06-19 16:10:00",
    "admission_end": "2169-06-22 19:45:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient with prior Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and known marginal ulcer history was admitted for 5 days of epigastric/right and left upper quadrant abdominal pain with nausea, diarrhea, and dark guaiac-positive stools. She received IV fluids and symptom control, and Gastroenterology performed EGD showing gastritis, a new small ulcer at the gastrojejunal anastomosis, improvement of a larger known anastomotic ulcer, and resolution of a previously seen smaller ulcer; pantoprazole was increased to 40 mg twice daily and sucralfate was continued. Stool testing was negative for H. pylori antigen and two C. difficile toxin tests were negative. CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast showed no bowel obstruction and noted a small left renal hypoattenuation focus without supportive urinalysis findings. Hematocrit was monitored for possible GI bleeding and stabilized/normalized prior to discharge; pain control was transitioned from IV morphine to oral hydromorphone with good effect. She was discharged home tolerating a regular diet, ambulating independently, with plans for repeat EGD in 6 weeks and surgical follow-up within 2 weeks, and return precautions for bleeding or worsening symptoms.",
    "problems": [
      "Gastrojejunal ulcer, unspecified as acute or chronic, without mention of hemorrhage or perforation, without mention of obstruction",
      "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult)(pediatric)",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Polycystic ovaries",
      "Bariatric surgery status"
    ]
  }
}
